{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>How goodly are your tents, O Jacob, your dwellings, O Israel.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he now prophesied concerning Israel's settlement in the land." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'How goodly are your tents, O Jacob' — concerning the tents in the wilderness, for Israel dwelt in tents, and because he saw that their openings were not aligned one opposite the other, he immediately said, 'How goodly are your tents.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'How goodly are your tents, O Jacob' — on high; and this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: the form of Jacob is engraved upon the Throne of Glory. And the word טובו is from the expression (Exodus 30:7) 'when he tends (בהטיבו) the lamps,' and אהליך is from the expression (Job 29:3) 'when his lamp shone (בהלו נרו) over me,' with an א added, and its meaning is 'light.' 'Your dwellings, O Israel' — below, for they draw strength from the טוב (good). And observe how he used the expression טובו with 'your tents,' and in connection with 'your dwellings' he immediately juxtaposed כנחלים נטיו ('like brooks they spread'), and the expression of נטיה applies to something physical, returning to 'your dwellings,' for the dwellings were spread out (נטוים)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:44.576720590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}